<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Namibia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as South West Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Namibia is a country in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, bordering the Atlantic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>. It was the German colony of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> from 1884, where the Herero and Nama genocide took place in 1904–08, and was ruled by South Africa from 1915. After a long liberation war led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> it became independent on 21 March 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/namibia.jpg" alt="Namibia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG by Vzb83 et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Namibia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Namibia covers 825,615 square kilometres and is the driest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. It borders the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> through the Caprivi Strip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Its landscape falls into five areas: the Central Plateau, the Namib Desert, the Great Escarpment, the Bushveld and the Kalahari Desert. The Namib, a hyper-arid expanse of gravel plains and dunes, runs along the whole coast and gives the country its name; Namib is a Khoi word meaning vast place. The Central Plateau holds the highest point, Königstein, at 2,573 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> as pastoralists and the San as hunter-gatherers lived in the dry lands for thousands of years, and Damara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nama-people">Nama peoples</a> also inhabited them. Around the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a> began to arrive, and from 1600 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Owambo</a> formed kingdoms such as Ondonga and Oukwanyama. The Portuguese navigators <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> in 1485 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> in 1486 were the first Europeans to land, but did not claim the area. From the late 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> people crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> from the Dutch Cape Colony, and conflict with the Herero led to the Nama-Herero War of 1880.</p><p>In 1878 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a> and the Penguin Islands, and in 1884 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> established rule over most of the territory as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>. Between 1904–08 German troops waged a punitive campaign against the Herero and Nama that escalated into what is often called the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the 20th century. South African forces defeated the Germans in 1915, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> mandated the colony to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> applied apartheid to the territory after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> won power in 1948. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> assumed direct responsibility in 1966, though South Africa kept de facto rule until 1973, when the UN recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> as the official representative of the Namibian people. Namibia became independent in March 1990 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a> and the Penguin Islands stayed under South African control until 1994. Namibia is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a>, and the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,022,401 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Namibia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First World War German forces from <b>South West Africa</b> took Naulila in December 1914, and several peoples revolted, among them the German-backed Ovambo Uprising of King Mandume ya Ndemufayo of Oukwanyama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in United Nations peacekeeping, and former president Martti Ahtisaari received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2008 for his mediation in <b>Namibia</b>, Aceh and Kosovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein, and its rule over <b>South West Africa</b>, which the United Nations later declared illegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Botha then led the campaign that forced the German surrender in <b>South West Africa</b> in July 1915, and South African troops fought in German East Africa under Smuts and on the Western Front, where the South African Brigade suffered heavy losses at the Battle of Delville Wood in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Damara were, with the San, among the first inhabitants of <b>Namibia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Egalitarian foragers who left rock paintings across the region, they were dispossessed and killed in large numbers by colonists from the 18th century, and in Botswana and <b>Namibia</b> they still contend for land and recognition.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
